# Architecture Standards

Most of this is enforced: dependency-cruiser (`pnpm arch:check` — layers, cycles, cross-feature internals, stack ports), `pnpm validate:structure` (folder/file whitelist in `projectStructure.config.cjs`), `pnpm validate:feature-size`, `pnpm validate:feature-docs`, and ESLint (`eslint.config.js`). Pre-commit runs the staged variants; CI runs the full ones. This rule covers what the tools can't judge.

## Proactive design checks

Each check runs **before** the code or plan step it guards, without waiting for the user to ask.

### Pattern risk (industry precedent — plans and proposals)

When a design may diverge from common industry practice for the capability — at any size — review it against precedent with [`loop-plan/references/review-precedent.md`](../../.agents/skills/loop-plan/references/review-precedent.md) (rubric: [`precedent-rubric.md`](../../.agents/skills/loop-plan/references/precedent-rubric.md); the agent picks the relevant aspects). This rule is the trigger; `loop-plan` and `loop-check` call it when it fires (skill-loop D56). Codebase convention alone does not justify divergence. Plans with material behavioral design fill **Pattern & precedent** (`.agents/skills/loop-plan/references/plan-template.md`); the size-M plan review has the same lens. Not for internal workaround shapes (§ Layer consistency) or package reuse (§ Don't reinvent the wheel).

### Don't reinvent the wheel (package / pattern reuse)

Before the first line of custom code for a generic subsystem (auth, webhooks, parsers, queues, rate limits, protocol clients, complex UI widgets, …), or before a plan locks a from-scratch approach, run [`.agents/skills/spec-reuse-package/SKILL.md`](../../.agents/skills/spec-reuse-package/SKILL.md). Skip for small, bespoke business logic. It recommends only: record the result in the plan (Conflict & compliance); do not install packages from it.

### Performance cost risk (heavy DB / UI operations)

When a step matches a heavy-operation trigger — N+1 / per-row queries, unbounded `SELECT`, missing index on a filtered/joined column, full scans or aggregates, round-trips in a loop, large lists without virtualization, unmemoized derived data over big arrays, main-thread blocking, oversized bundles:

1. Estimate the cost (rows, round-trips, render work).
2. Design a cheaper option first (narrower query + index, pagination, server-side aggregation, caching via query keys, batching, virtualization, memoization, `startTransition`); prefer the reductive option (`.claude/rules/debugging.md` § Reductive Strategy).
3. Implement the cheaper option and record the trade-off in the plan or PR summary. Do not ship the expensive path silently. Ask only when the choice falls on `.claude/rules/agent-behavior.md` § When to ask (for example, paid usage or a data or irreversible change).

Tools: CWV symptoms → `web-perf` plugin skill; structural fix → `.agents/skills/spec-optimize-performance/SKILL.md`; stack-native fixes → `.agents/skills/spec-optimize-performance/SKILL.md`.

### Layer consistency (workaround guard)

**Core reflex:** every request carries the user's assumption about how the system works — they can't see the implementation, so verify it before acting.

**Proactively** on any request to change existing behavior ("add a color to the gradient", "just add a field/button/flag"), minimizer/exception language, a first-of-a-kind instance without a category, or behavior-by-analogy. **Reactively** when the diff takes a workaround shape (special-case flag, parallel path, symptom tuning, disconnected patch, instance-encoded naming, growing scope, "for now" comment).

Run [`.agents/skills/spec-check-layer/SKILL.md`](../../.agents/skills/spec-check-layer/SKILL.md) (shapes: [`references/workaround-shapes.md`](../../.agents/skills/spec-check-layer/references/workaround-shapes.md)) before writing the workaround. New or copied identifiers always follow `.claude/rules/code-style.md` § Naming — the one-off skip does not suppress it. For other shapes apply the severity filter; when one clears it, post the **WARNING, WORKAROUND** block and **stop** until the owner picks workaround vs structural fix (stop list: patch on top, `agent-behavior` § When to ask). When the precedent review also applies, run this first (`.agents/skills/router/references/tiebreaks.md`).

## Code placement

Layer placement, import direction, barrels and the placement decision order: `.claude/rules/app-code.md` § Code placement (loads with `src/**`).

## Folder structure

Find the real layout with `ls src/`. Allowed paths: `projectStructure.config.cjs`; check a new one with `pnpm validate:structure`. Annotated tree for humans: `ARCHITECTURE.md` § Folder Structure. Neither tool says what a folder means:

- `components/common/` — presentation-only UI kit, no business logic, no feature imports.
- `features/<name>/` — one bounded domain concept (§ Feature granularity), `README.md` required.
- `shared/` — reused by 2+ features.
- `layouts/` — app shell; may compose features.

Backend runtimes (if ever added) live in `cloud-functions/<service>/`.

**Path aliases (SSOT):** `@/*` → `src/*` is the only alias (`tsconfig.app.json`). Mirror the filesystem (`@/shared/utils/<module>`, `@/features/<feature>/hooks/<hook>`). No `../` parent imports, no bare aliases like `@shared`.

### Edge-case placement

Edge-case placement (layout hooks, schemas, state, guards, toasts, …): `.claude/rules/app-code.md` § Edge-case placement.

## Feature granularity / bounded context

**One feature = one bounded domain concept** (one reason to change, one vocabulary). Layer rules alone don't stop a monolithic feature. If you can name two independent domain concepts in one feature (e.g. game _world_, _combat_, _inventory_), split them; move cross-cutting engine code to `src/shared/`.

- **Agent obligation:** before creating files for new work, enumerate the domain concepts and split in the plan when there is more than one or the projected size exceeds budgets — don't wait to be asked.
- **Budgets (SSOT `featureBudgets.config.cjs`):** `defaults.maxFilesPerLayer`, `defaults.maxFilesPerFeature`, `overrides.<feature>` (non-empty `reason` required), `baseline.<feature>` (grandfathered; regenerate with `pnpm validate:feature-size:baseline`).
- **Cross-feature imports:** never deep-import another feature's internals; use its barrel (`index.ts`) or `api/` layer, or extract to `src/shared/` (dependency-cruiser `no-cross-feature-internals*`).

## Plain optimistic + server-canonical response (TanStack Query)

Default for mutations that must feel instant: optimistic `onMutate` with rollback, then `setQueryData` with the mutation's canonical result — no `invalidateQueries`/`refetch` on the happy path (flashes). Playbook, exceptions, example (SSOT): `documentation/DOC_TANSTACK_QUERY.md` § Plain optimistic + server-canonical response.

## Changing architecture rules and configs

Violations mean the code is wrong, not the rule. Report the violation, explain what the rule protects, and fix the code. Do not edit rules, `projectStructure.config.cjs`, `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`, or baselines to absorb a violation. If the user asks for a rule change, warn that it weakens consistency and enforcement across the app, proceed only once they confirm, and record why (protected-file consent: `.claude/rules/agent-behavior.md`).

Legitimate cross-layer exception (rare): regenerate the dependency-cruiser baseline (`pnpm arch:baseline` — protected, ask first) and justify it in the PR. Existing violations are ratcheted: baselines ignore known ones; new ones must be fixed.

## Architecture docs

- `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` live in the repo root.
- Update `ARCHITECTURE.md` after structural changes; feature docs live in `src/features/*/README.md` (pre-commit requires staging it with feature changes — `documentation/DOC_FEATURE_LOCAL_README.md`).
- `src/features/*/docs/*.md` only with explicit user approval; temporary plans go in `documentation/jobs/` or `documentation/temp/`.
